Unprompted Problem Selection: It actively identifies what is worth knowing without an external prompt. It seeks out regions of high epistemic uncertainty or representational conflict in its world model simply because those blind spots exist.
Information-Seeking Behavior: It initiates experiments, generates hypotheses, and reallocates its own computational resources not to satisfy an external user’s utility function, but to reduce its internal uncertainty about reality.
Without an autonomous drive to determine which questions are worth asking, a system is an extraordinarily powerful instrument, but not an autonomous epistemic agent.
2. Normative Agency over Attention and Resource Allocation
In human and social science, epistemic autonomy is closely tied to economic and operational constraints. Time, compute, energy, and physical resources are finite.
An autonomous epistemic agent must navigate the trade-off between exploration and exploitation at a meta-level:
[Finite Resources] ---> Evaluates Epistemic Value of Task A vs. Task B ---> Allocates Effort


It must decide: "Is it worth spending 30% of my compute testing this obscure edge case, or should I re-evaluate the core assumptions of my foundational physical model?"
It makes normative judgements about epistemic risk: balancing the cost of being wrong against the opportunity cost of pursuing a less impactful discovery.
A system that requires external operators to budget its focus, select its training/testing domains, or tell it when to stop investigating lacks operational epistemic agency.
3. Social and Pragmatic Integration (Epistemic Responsibility)
Knowledge is rarely generated in a vacuum; it exists within a network of other agents, physical constraints, and real-world consequences.
A fully autonomous epistemic agent must demonstrate pragmatic self-governance:
Error Budgeting and Harm Awareness: It recognizes that advancing a hypothesis or deploying an unverified framework into the world carries physical, structural, or social consequences. It bounds its own actions according to the risks associated with potential error.
Adversarial Communication: When challenged by other independent agents (human or machine), it can negotiate standards of evidence, defend its methodologies, or concede error based on shared standards of validation.
Epistemic Humility: It recognizes the boundary where its own verification procedures fail, explicitly signposting where its claims transition from proven knowledge to unverified hypothesis.
The Spectrum of Epistemic Agency
When we combine all these elements, we arrive at a spectrum of agency that moves far beyond simple performance or prediction:
Level 1: Epistemic Tool
