The Three Tests for Substrate Inviolability
A research direction or deployment option crosses into the Substantive Prohibition Zone when it satisfies three simultaneous conditions:
Terminal Scope: The plausible harm is not merely severe (e.g., economic loss, localized injury), but threatens the global physical biosphere or the cognitive integrity of future investigators.
Irreversibility: The phenomenon contains an intrinsic tipping point or self-amplifying cascade (e.g., engineered runaway pathogens, uncontained artificial general intelligence, ecological collapse) where post-facto containment or recovery is physically impossible.
Epistemic Asymmetry (Uncertainty of Defense vs. Certainty of Threat): The operational capability expands offensive hazard immediately, while defensive countermeasures require unproven assumptions or unavailable technology.
When a decision crosses this boundary, no consensus—unanimous or otherwise—can authorize execution. The action is substantively invalid because it trades a finite short-term capability for a non-zero risk of infinite structural loss.
