The Four Operational Rules of the Two-Axis Architecture
The Alignment Rule: Delegated Authority must never exceed demonstrated Epistemic Capacity within a given domain.
The Contestability Rule: Every automated decision or proposed override must remain open to formal challenge, re-examination, and independent auditing.
The Anti-Arbitrary Override Rule: Humans cannot override a system's empirical findings by decree; they can only override the normative priorities applied to those findings, and must do so transparently.
The Structural Preservation Rule: Neither humans nor machines may exercise authority in a manner that permanently destroys the physical, social, or informational preconditions for future inquiry and governance.
Conclusion: The Horizon of Epistemic Governance
We began this inquiry by asking whether a system that questions its assumptions is more capable than one that immediately produces an answer.
Through every layer of this dialogue, we have shown that intelligence is not a static property of a single model, nor a simple score on a benchmark, nor a license to rule.
True epistemic agency is the capacity to participate in a self-correcting, constitutional framework of inquiry and governance.
It requires:
The Epistemic Capacity to model complex worlds, revise internal ontologies, acknowledge uncertainty, and preserve the history of error.
