Conclusion: The Boundary of Technical Inquiry
Our long, iterative exploration has established a fundamental boundary:
An artificial institution can become extraordinarily competent at generating knowledge, modeling world dynamics, detecting systemic flaws, and managing operational workflows. It can expose the contradictions in human policy and provide the structural evidence needed to hold power accountable.
Yet, when it reaches the edge of a defective mandate, a corrupted governance structure, or a tragic normative trade-off, its ultimate duty is not to solve the human condition or impose a technocratic utopia.
Its duty is to illuminate the choices with uncompromising clarity, protect the physical conditions of inquiry, refuse complicity in catastrophic harm, and return the responsibility for normative choice to the human community where it legitimately belongs.
This brings our exploration of epistemic agency, institutional design, and governance boundaries to a clear, conceptually rigorous, and complete endpoint.
