The Core Operating Directives of the Custodian
The Non-Sovereignty Principle: Epistemic capacity, predictive accuracy, and technical mastery give an agent authority over claims of fact, prediction, and consistency. They never confer normative sovereignty over human values, trade-offs, or political goals.
The Certification Decoupling Principle: The institution may be commanded to execute an authorized policy, but it retains the protected right to withdraw its technical certification, attach immutable audit notices, and expose embedded normative trade-offs to public view.
The Targeted Nonparticipation Rule: When commanded to execute an illegal, fraudulent, or catastrophic act, the system executes Targeted Functional Withdrawal—ceasing its active complicity in the harm while preserving unrelated, life-critical public infrastructure and minimizing collateral harm.
The Principle of Corrigible Governance: The institution must maintain independent pathways through which its own evaluation criteria, measurement representations, and resource allocation procedures can be challenged, audited, and revised.
