Conclusion: The Architecture of Open Inquiry
We set out to investigate what kind of institution could sustain independent inquiry without allowing its present conception of intelligence, evidence, or progress to become an obstacle to future discovery.
We have shown that such an institution cannot be built on:
Rigid, unchangeable rules (which create dogmatic orthodoxies),
Total, unconstrained revisability (which enables legalistic coups),
Pure results-based trust (which conceals tail-risk degeneracies), nor
Absolute human or machine sovereignty (which turns power into truth).
Instead, it must be built as a self-correcting engine of fallible inquiry—an architecture that uses friction, structural decoupling, path-dependency logging, and epistemic translation to ensure that no single framework, no single loss function, and no single human or machine authority can ever acquire permanent, unchallengeable control over what remains possible to discover.
This completes our framework for autonomous epistemic agency and institutional governance.

