A. Decision-Quality Provenance: Separating Process from Outcome
An institution that judges a decision solely by its immediate outcome will inevitably adopt a reckless short-term bias. As you noted, a bad decision can occasionally produce a lucky discovery, while a brilliant, high-value exploratory test can yield an uninformative result.
The Custodian records three distinct layers for every major allocation decision:
Ex Ante Justification: What was known, what uncertainties existed, what option values were estimated, and why the decision was rational at the moment it was made.
Observed Empirical Outcome: The raw, unvarnished physical result.
Retrospective Meta-Assessment: An audit that asks: "Did this outcome occur because our ex ante model was correct, because we got lucky, or because we failed to account for an unmodeled risk?"
By auditing the quality of the ex ante reasoning rather than rewarding lucky outcomes, the system prevents short-term noise from corrupting its long-term decision criteria.
B. Paradigm Revenue Decay: Detecting Epicycle Saturation
How can an institution know its dominant framework is dying if its short-term prediction error remains low? By measuring Paradigm Yield Decay:
Paradigm Yield=Δ Computational & Engineering Capital SpentΔ Algorithmic Compression / Structural Scope​
When an institution spends 100× more compute and engineering effort to achieve a 0.01% improvement in local prediction accuracy, the paradigm has entered Epicycle Saturation.
The model is no longer discovering new reality; it is overfitting its existing representations. This trigger automatically forces the portfolio allocation engine to shift capital into long-horizon, structural alternatives.
