User prompt: I agree with the diagnosis of epistemic myopia via feedback asymmetry, but I think the proposed remedies reveal a deeper problem: we are trying to protect research whose value is difficult to measure by constructing better ways to measure it. That is partly necessary. But there is a danger that we simply move the optimization pressure one level up. If the institution tracks paradigm yield, researchers may optimize for conceptual novelty. If it tracks long-horizon milestones, they may redefine milestones to make slow projects appear productive. If it rewards good ex ante reasoning, it may produce increasingly sophisticated justifications for decisions that are difficult to evaluate. This is not an argument against measurement. It is an argument for recognizing that an institution can become very good at demonstrating that it follows a sound research process without becoming correspondingly better at discovering anything important. I want to explore three consequences. 1. Paradigm Yield Decay is a warning signal, not a verdict Your proposed measure of paradigm yield is useful as a diagnostic: 
ParadigmYield=StructuralScopeorCompressionResearchResources
