arch that takes decades to mature cannot reasonably be evaluated using monthly prediction error. However, a protected time horizon can solve one problem while creating another. Suppose a research program has operated for fifteen years. Its proponents argue that its central ideas require another fifteen years before meaningful validation becomes possible. They may be correct. But they may also be protecting an increasingly unproductive program from scrutiny by continually postponing the point at which its claims can be tested. How should the institution distinguish these cases? I would separate protection from inappropriate evaluation from exemption from evaluation. A long-horizon program should not be required to produce rapid results merely because other programs can. It should still periodically demonstrate that its research strategy remains coherent, that its assumptions are explicit, and that the next stage of work has a defensible relationship to the knowledge it seeks to establish. Those assessments need not demand discoveries on a predetermined schedule. They might instead examine whether the program has: - reduced a particular uncertainty; - developed a previously unavailable method; - established that a proposed approach is less promising than initially believed; - identified a discriminating experiment; - or clarified why the original research question needs reformulation. An unsuccessful investigation can produce genuine knowledge. Conversely, fifteen years of activity can produce very little. The distinction is not between successful and unsuccessful projects. It is between projects that are learning something about their problem and projects whose continued operation no longer has an adequately justified relationship to that problem. Even this distinction will sometimes remain uncertain. That is why long-horizon protection should be periodically reconsiderable rather than permanent. 3. Some research has option value that cannot be estimated in advance This is where I think our framework encounters its hardest resource-allocation problem. Imagine an institution considering three projects: - Project A offers reliable, measurable improvements to an existing technology. - Project B explores a plausible extension of the current framework. - Project C investigates an unfamiliar approach whose potential applications cannot yet be specified. A conventional portfolio model might estimate expected returns, account for uncertainty, and allocate resources accordingly. But Project C presents a special difficulty: its value may consist partly in creating capabilities, questions, or conceptual categories that the institution does not yet know how to evaluate. We can call this epistemic option value: the value of retaining the practical ability to pursue future discoveries that are not yet sufficiently understood to estimate their full consequences. This is not the same as saying that unknown possibilities deserve unlimited resources. Every imaginable project has some conceivable future benefit, and that fact alone cannot justify funding it. Instead, the institution must consider whether a relatively modest investment can preserve a meaningful future opportunity at a reasonable cost. A small exploratory experiment might be worthwhile even when its immediate expected return is low, because it could determine whether a much larger research direction deserves attention. A dormant project might merit preservation because reconstructing its methods later would be disproportionately expensive. Another project might have so little plausible connection to a useful discovery that its option value does not justify even a small investment. The difficult part is that these judgments cannot always be reduced to reliable numerical estimates. I would therefore avoid making epistemic option value another score to maximize. It should be a reason to consider a decision, with the reasoning and uncertainty recorded explicitly. This also suggests that the institution should distinguish between funding a research program and funding a cheap test of whether the program deserves to exist. Those are very different commitments. The second can preserve opportunities without granting every speculative idea a permanent claim on institutional resources. 4. The institution must learn from what it never investigated Your Path-Dependency Ledger records research avenues that were denied resources or retired before their assumptions could be tested. But there is a deeper limitation: an institution can record only possibilities it has somehow identified. What about the research direction that never occurred to any of its investigators? What about the phenomenon excluded from its data collection because its current theory offered no reason to measure it? What about the conceptual breakthrough that could have emerged only through a research tradition the institution never developed? These omissions leave no straightforward entry in the ledger. This creates an asymmetry between known uncertainty and unknown uncertainty. Known uncertainty can be documented, prioritized, and investigated. Unknown uncertainty is harder: the institution may not even possess the vocabulary needed to describe what it is missing. No audit can guarantee the discovery of every blind spot. We should be cautious about implying otherwise. However, the institution can preserve conditions that make such discoveries more likely. It can expose researchers to alternative methods, permit unexpected observations to generate new research questions, support investigators who challenge established problem formulations, and periodically examine whether its measurement and selection practices systematically exclude entire classes of inquiry. The goal is not to enumerate every possible future discovery. It is to avoid making the institution's present conception of discoverability a permanent boundary around its future. That is a more modest ambition than eliminating epi