To resolve this without causing institutional paralysis, we must separate the decision into four distinct risk-bearing categories:
1. The Decision-Maker (Capacity & Authority Loss)
If an artificial or human sub-agent exercises Level 3 Bounded Discretion to override an established rule or execute a high-risk probe, its decision-making authority in that domain shrinks proportionally to the irreversibility of the risk.
If the decision proves catastrophically wrong, the sub-agent does not simply say "it was a rational gamble." It loses the right to exercise Level 3 discretion in that domain without multi-lateral authorization.
2. The Institution (Carrying Cost & Insurance)
The institution bears the remedial and financial burden of preservation. If an institution chooses to execute a probe that consumes a unique sample or risks an operational outage, it must set aside an explicit Epistemic Recovery Reserve (compute, data redundancy, physical containment) to mitigate the loss if the gamble fails.
3. The Affected Stakeholders (Substantive Protection & Consent)
When an irreversible risk threatens non-participating humans or biospheres, no amount of internal institutional necessity can override their right to substantive protection.
The risk cannot be transferred to affected stakeholders without their explicit, informed representation or pre-established constitutional limits (Substrate Inviolability).
4. The Future Investigators (Path-Dependency Indemnification)
Future investigators lose options when current decisions consume unique resources or retire paradigms. The institution indemnifies future investigators not by promising never to consume resources, but by maintaining the Path-Dependency Ledger and Conflict Legibility record.
Future investigators inherit the loss, but they do not inherit the lie that the option was scientifically disproved. They receive the exact structural record showing it was sacrificed due to scarcity and high-risk trade-offs.
