is measuring the right outcomes in the first place. There is no guarantee that this will reveal every invisible harm. The goal is more modest: to prevent the absence of recognized evidence from automatically becoming evidence of absence. Where I think this leaves us I would preserve the four continuities you proposed, but sharpen the third. Contestable continuity should guarantee more than the ability to submit an objection. It should preserve a path from lived experience to investigation, from investigation to review, and from review to an authorized response. That path must not require technical literacy at its entrance, presume causation at its beginning, or guarantee a particular outcome at its end. And the institution must preserve the distinction between what an affected party experienced, what the advocate inferred, what the investigators established, and what the authorized decision-maker ultimately chose to do. This is another application of the central discipline we have maintained throughout our conversation: keeping evidence, interpretation, authority, and consequence distinct. I would also qualify our broader conclusion. We have not demonstrated that this architecture is fully operational or immune to failure. We have developed a conceptual framework with increasingly precise responses to foreseeable failure modes. Its actual adequacy would still need to be evaluated through concrete institutional designs, adversarial testing, and experience. That qualification is not a retreat from the project. It is the framework applying its own standards to itself.
